  n<//----------------------------------------------------------------------> u<2> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<3> l<1:1> el<1:73>
  n<// Srdy/drdy round-robin arbiter> u<3> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<4> l<2:1> el<2:33>
  n<// but without the one cycle decision delay.> u<4> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<5> l<3:1> el<3:45>
  n<//> u<5> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<6> l<4:1> el<4:3>
  n<// This component supports multiple round-robin modes:> u<6> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<7> l<5:1> el<5:55>
  n<//> u<7> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<8> l<6:1> el<6:3>
  n<// Mode 0 : Each input gets a single cycle, regardless of data> u<8> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<9> l<7:1> el<7:63>
  n<//          availability.  This mode functions like a TDM> u<9> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<10> l<8:1> el<8:58>
  n<//          demultiplexer.  Output flow control will cause the> u<10> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<11> l<9:1> el<9:63>
  n<//          component to stall, so that inputs do not miss their> u<11> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<12> l<10:1> el<10:65>
  n<//          turn due to flow control.> u<12> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<13> l<11:1> el<11:38>
  n<// Mode 0 fast arb : Each input gets a single grant. If the> u<13> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<14> l<12:1> el<12:60>
  n<//          output is not ready (p_drdy deasserted), then the> u<14> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<15> l<13:1> el<13:62>
  n<//          machine will hold on that particular input until it> u<15> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<16> l<14:1> el<14:64>
  n<//          receives a grant.  Once a single token has been> u<16> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<17> l<15:1> el<15:60>
  n<//          accepted the machine will round-robin arbitrate.> u<17> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<18> l<16:1> el<16:61>
  n<//          When there are no requests the machine returns to> u<18> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<19> l<17:1> el<17:62>
  n<//          its default state.> u<19> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<20> l<18:1> el<18:31>
  n<// Mode 1 : Each input can transmit for as long as it has data.> u<20> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<21> l<19:1> el<19:64>
  n<//          When input deasserts, device will begin to hunt for a> u<21> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<22> l<20:1> el<20:66>
  n<//          new input with data.> u<22> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<23> l<21:1> el<21:33>
  n<// Mode 2 : Continue to accept input until the incoming data> u<23> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<24> l<22:1> el<22:61>
  n<//          matches a particular "end pattern".  The end pattern> u<24> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<25> l<23:1> el<23:65>
  n<//          is provided on the c_rearb (re-arbitrate) input.  When> u<25> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<26> l<24:1> el<24:67>
  n<//          c_rearb is high, will hunt for new inputs on next clock.> u<26> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<27> l<25:1> el<25:69>
  n<//> u<27> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<28> l<26:1> el<26:3>
  n<// This component also supports two arbitration modes: slow and fast.> u<28> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<29> l<27:1> el<27:70>
  n<// slow rotates the grant from requestor to requestor cycle by cycle,> u<29> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<30> l<28:1> el<28:70>
  n<// so each requestor gets serviced at most once every #inputs cycles.> u<30> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<31> l<29:1> el<29:70>
  n<// This can be useful for producing a TDM-type interface, however> u<31> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<32> l<30:1> el<30:66>
  n<// requestors may be delayed waiting for the grant to come around even> u<32> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<33> l<31:1> el<31:71>
  n<// if there are no other requestors.> u<33> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<34> l<32:1> el<32:37>
  n<//> u<34> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1971> s<35> l<33:1> el<33:3>
